            <div xml:id="d478">

                <pb facs="0547" n="535" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg535"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">859.01/162: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hamilton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-05-12T14:00:00+03:00">May 12, 1944—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1672. Embassy 1651, May 11, 3 p.m.<anchor n="28" xml:id="d478-f1"/> The British
                    Ambassador did not inform the Soviets of the approach to the King of Denmark and
                    I also did not. I assume, reference Department’s 1168, May 11, 3 p.m.,<anchor
                        n="28" xml:id="d478-f2"/> that if the British Foreign Office agrees that the
                    Soviets should now be informed of the approach to the King of Denmark, I shall
                    receive further instructions, in the event that the Department desires that both
                    the British and ourselves communicate this information to the Soviet Foreign
                    Office.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to London as 108.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hamilton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
